The extended-real Hölder norm is
\[
\|f\|_{H^s}
=
\max\left\{
\max_{|\kappa|\le\lceil s\rceil-1}\|\partial^\kappa f\|_\infty,\;
\max_{|\kappa|=\lceil s\rceil-1}
\sup_{x\ne x'}
\frac{|\partial^\kappa f(x)-\partial^\kappa f(x')|}
{\|x-x'\|_2^{\,s-\lceil s\rceil+1}}
\right\},
\]
where \(\partial^\kappa f\) denotes the partial derivative indexed by the multi-index \(\kappa\). Its value is \(+\infty\) if the indicated continuous derivatives do not exist.

dcd3b4e638ebabbc20b4c8cabe514594e32d459b.body:
\[
\widehat T_{h,J}=
\begin{cases}
\mathrm{clip}_{[-3/4,3/4]}
\left(r_0^\top\widehat Q_{h,J}^{-1}\widehat R_{h,J}\right),
&\lambda_{\min}(\widehat Q_{h,J})\ge 3/32,\\
0,&\lambda_{\min}(\widehat Q_{h,J})<3/32.
\end{cases}
\]
Here \(r_0\) is the coarse polynomial basis vector evaluated at the target profile, \(\widehat R_{h,J}\) is the empirical response vector, and \(\widehat Q_{h,J}\) is the symmetrized empirical matrix. The function \(\mathrm{clip}_{[-3/4,3/4]}\) projects a real value onto \([-3/4,3/4]\), and \(\lambda_{\min}\) denotes the smallest eigenvalue.

7379df6fe210c61278158c93952dee00a6973e48.body:
The extended-real Hölder norm \(\|f\|_{H^s}\), for \(f:\mathbb R^d\to\mathbb R\), \(d\in\{0,1,\ldots\}\), and \(s\in\mathbb R\), is
\[
\|f\|_{H^s}
=
\begin{cases}
\displaystyle
\max\left\{
\sup_{\substack{\kappa\in\{0,1,\ldots\}^d,\;
|\kappa|\le \max\{\lceil s\rceil-1,0\}\\
x\in[0,1]^d}}
|\partial^\kappa f(x)|,\;
\sup_{\substack{\kappa\in\{0,1,\ldots\}^d,\;
|\kappa|=\max\{\lceil s\rceil-1,0\}\\
x,x'\in[0,1]^d,\;x\ne x'}}
\frac{|\partial^\kappa f(x)-\partial^\kappa f(x')|}
{\|x-x'\|_2^{\,s-\max\{\lceil s\rceil-1,0\}}}
\right\},
& f\in C^{\max\{\lceil s\rceil-1,0\}}([0,1]^d),\\[1ex]
+\infty,
& \text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}
\]
Here \(|\kappa|=\sum_{i=1}^d\kappa_i\), and \(\partial^\kappa f(x)\) is the iterated derivative within \([0,1]^d\), evaluated on coordinate directions with coordinate \(i\) repeated \(\kappa_i\) times. The differentiability condition means continuous differentiability of the indicated order within the cube. Both suprema are taken in the extended nonnegative reals, with an empty supremum equal to zero.
